{#
  ICON — the single source for inline SVG.

  Every glyph in the site is defined once in the `paths` map below and rendered
  through this macro. Nothing else in the codebase should paste raw <svg> markup:
  the old index.html shipped the same 700-byte chevron five separate times, and
  every one of them had to be edited by hand to change a colour.

  PROPS
    name    key in the map below (required)
    size    px, applied to width AND height (default 24)
    rotate  degrees, for reusing one glyph in several directions (default 0)
    class   extra classes on the <svg>
    title   accessible name; when omitted the icon is aria-hidden (decorative)

  USAGE
    {% raw %}{% from "components/icon.njk" import icon %}
    {{ icon("chevron-down") }}
    {{ icon("chevron-down", rotate=90, title="Next project") }}{% endraw %}
#}

{#
  THE WORDMARK is in this map rather than an <img>, and that is load-bearing.
  The header flips between a light and a dark variant (Figma 446:37196), so
  the mark has to recolour with it — and an <img> cannot: SVG loaded that way
  is an isolated document that the page's CSS and `currentColor` never reach.
  Inline, it inherits `color` from .site-header__logo like every other glyph.

  THREE PATHS, NOT TWO. The mark was reissued as Figma component 528:68221
  ("logo-header"); the third path is the swept tail that turns the P silhouette
  into more of an R. Assets/images/logo-header.svg is the same artwork as a
  standalone file, also in currentColor — that file is the source of truth for
  this entry, so if the mark is redrawn again, update both together.

  Assets/images/hero/logo-mark.svg is the OLD two-path artwork with fill="black"
  baked in. It is still what the OG/social tags point at, where a fixed colour is
  correct and a stale-by-one-tail mark is survivable, so that file stays.
#}
{#
  THE MOBILE MENU BUTTON — 566:37653, which names iconamoon's
  menu-burger-horizontal-duotone. DRAWN TO MATCH, NOT EXTRACTED: the vector could
  not be pulled (get_design_context is blocked by the Figma MCP's
  allowed-directories setting), so "menu-burger" below is three even bars on the
  node's 24 grid. If the real glyph is ever exported and its bars differ in
  length or opacity, replace that `d` and nothing else needs to change.

  AND A NOTE ON COMMENTS IN THIS FILE: the map below is a Nunjucks expression,
  not JS. A C-style comment inside those braces is parsed as a division operator
  and the build dies with "unexpected token: /". Notes about a glyph belong up
  here in a template comment, never between the entries — and take care not to
  write a closing comment marker inside one, which ends it early and breaks the
  build the same way.
#}
{#
  THE THREE BRAND CHIPS — 610:60749 / 610:60737 / 610:60701, which the footer
  node names as brandico:linkedin-rect, fa7-brands:square-dribbble and
  fa:behance-square. All three are the SQUARE variants: the rounded tile is part
  of the glyph, not a background the CSS adds, which is why each entry draws its
  own tile and knocks the mark out of it.

  LINKEDIN AND BEHANCE ARE DRAWN TO MATCH, NOT EXTRACTED — the same position
  "menu-burger" is in, and for the same reason: get_design_context cannot run
  while the Figma MCP's allowed-directories list is empty. Both are built to the
  public brand marks on the node's 24 grid and checked against a render of
  610:60761. If their true vectors are ever exported, replacing those two `d`
  values is the whole job.

  DRIBBBLE IS THE REAL THING, exported by hand from Figma and normalised here.
  Three attempts at drawing it failed in a way the other two did not, and the
  export explains why: the ball's three seams are not lines at all. The source is
  a single path on a 28 grid where the tile, an oppositely-wound circle and six
  filled surface patches combine under nonzero winding — the seams are the GAPS
  left between the patches, so no amount of stroking a circle was ever going to
  land. Split into this file's two-colour convention it becomes three layers
  painted in order: tile, then the circle knocked out, then the patches put back.
  Coordinates are the export's, scaled x6/7 to the 24 grid at 4dp — the ball
  arrives at centre (12,12) r 8.5714, so it self-centres.

  THE TILE IS THIS FILE'S, NOT THE EXPORT'S. The export rounds its 28px square at
  r4 (14.3%); the other two chips round a 24px square at r4.8 (20%). Taking the
  export wholesale would have given one chip of three visibly sharper corners, so
  only the ball and its patches were lifted.

  TWO COLOURS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS A VARIABLE. The tile is currentColor so it
  inherits like every other glyph here; the mark is punched out in
  --icon-knockout, which _footer.css points at the band's own --surface. The
  fallback is #000 because that is what a white tile on a dark page needs if the
  variable never arrives. Do not switch this to fill-rule:evenodd on one path —
  the knockout then follows the tile's colour and the chip cannot sit on any
  surface but black.
#}
